title: 1. On arrival, Top asks four questions. A request that answers no to all four goes straight to its owner as one building mission, unplanned.
pid: 2d90388e-1975-493d-bcda-39db7647234b
body: |2-
  - Is there more than one reasonable way to do it, and does the choice
    matter? A plan that would only say "this is how we will build it"
    is not worth writing.
  - Does it cross a border: two territories, or one of the "three
    borders that do not follow files" in [[id:f677a22d-0dc2-4c07-95c8-82db97739a62][the team that builds skg]]?
    Minds that work on one change one after another each make choices
    the other cannot see, and the choices conflict.
  - Does it pass a one-way door: what is written to disk, the wire
    protocol, what becomes visible in a public skgrepo, a feature
    removed, anything by which Jeff's data could be lost?
  - Will it take more than one building mission? A mind that tries to
    finish a large thing in one session runs out of room and leaves
    it half done for the next.
  Most requests answer no to all four, and planning those is the
  costly error. An unplanned request still gets a mission that says
  what done looks like (step 7).
  For a planned request, Top writes in the planning mission an
  appetite: how many building missions the request is worth. It is a
  budget, not an estimate; the plan shapes the work to fit it.
  Rests on: read (Google's design docs; Cognition; Bezos's one-way
  doors; Rust's "substantial" changes; Anthropic's long-running
  harnesses; Anthropic's research system, whose early versions spent
  fifty agents on simple questions; Shape Up's appetite). The four
  questions as a set are ours, and ? (unsure: the threshold).
  The four answers are also the request's first tier (step 4).
